makepad remote bridge: a per-launch token, browser requests refused, no wildcard CORS (decision D10c).

The bridge (`--remote` / MAKEPAD_REMOTE) injects real input, serves screen grabs and returns every TextInput's raw
buffer. It answered every request with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` and checked nothing, so any web page (and any
local process or, on a phone, any other app) could read /snap and fire /click on a running app. This patch:
- requires a per-launch secret on EVERY request, header `X-Makepad-Token`, compared in constant time (403
  {"err":"token"} otherwise). The token is MAKEPAD_REMOTE_TOKEN when given (at run time, or baked in at build time on
  phones, like the port), else `mprt_` + 32 bytes from /dev/urandom as hex (the prefix lets repo_hermetic find a leaked one); it is written to `<tmp>/makepad-remote/port-<PORT>.token`
  (dir 0700, file 0600) for the harness, and never printed or logged (the listening line names only the file);
- refuses (403 {"err":"browser"}) any request carrying `Origin`, `Referer` or `Sec-Fetch-*` (every browser request
  carries one; curl, urllib and the harness send none), and a `Host` naming a domain other than localhost (DNS
  rebinding; IP literals stay allowed for fleet boxes);
- stops sending `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`.
Applied to every tree by scripts/apply-makepad-patches.sh; tests/makepad_bridge_guard.rs checks its shape.
Clients: tools/walk/bridgeauth.py (urllib) and harness/bcurl (curl) send the token for a loopback port.

